WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:03.200
Narrator: Look at this
famous deleted scene

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.630
in "Terminator 2: Judgment Day."

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:07.840
We're looking directly
at Arnold Schwarzenegger

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.910
and Linda Hamilton's reflections,

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:13.370
but we can't see the
camera capturing it all.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:16.020
That's because that's not really a mirror

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.580
and this reflection of
Hamilton isn't actually her.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.330
Filmmakers have mastered the art

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.120
of hiding their cameras from a mirror

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.420
using visual trickery and
precise cameras and choreography.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.550
Today, they can shoot mirrors in ways

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.883
that once seemed impossible.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.700
Innovative mirror shots stretch back

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.610
to the beginning of film,

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.240
like when Orson Welles captured

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:43.980
the now-iconic fun-house-mirror sequence

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.100
in 1947's "The Lady from Shanghai."

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.740
Of the 80 mirrors in the sequence,

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.400
some were actually two-way mirrors.

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.370
While Welles and Rita Hayworth acted

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:54.960
on the reflective side of the glass,

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.490
the camera could shoot everything

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.900
from the non-reflective
side without being detected.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.317
Two-way mirrors are still useful today.

00:01:02.317 --> 00:01:04.060
"It Chapter Two" used them

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.310
for its terrifying fun-house sequence

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:09.970
to switch between the reflections
of this kid and Pennywise.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:11.361
But a convincing mirror shot

00:01:11.361 --> 00:01:14.544
doesn't necessarily need
to use a real mirror.

00:01:14.544 --> 00:01:16.220
Like that scene from "Terminator 2"

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:19.303
where Sarah Connor removes the
chip from the T-800's head.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.820
The camera was directly
behind the characters,

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.430
but it's nowhere to be
seen in the reflection.

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:28.930
That's because for this trick,

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:30.960
the mirror is actually a window

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.800
looking into a duplicate
version of the set.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.150
Performers stand on either
side of the reflection

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:38.080
mimicking each other's movements.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.970
So Hamilton can be seen here,

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:43.350
but her reflection is actually
the actress's twin sister

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:44.980
next to Schwarzenegger.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:47.310
And Schwarzenegger's
silhouette in the foreground?

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:50.120
Well, that's a very lifelike dummy.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:53.290
The body-double trick works
best with twin actors.

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:54.830
Otherwise, filmmakers have to make sure

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:56.480
to avoid the double's face,

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:59.660
as seen in this mirror shot
from 1995's "La Haine."

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:02.610
And this technique is
also prone to human error.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.230
Take this shot from 2011's "Sucker Punch."

00:02:05.230 --> 00:02:07.100
If you look closely,
you'll notice the timing

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:10.320
of the actresses and their
doubles is slightly off.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.160
To avoid this issue and
let actors focus on acting

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:17.340
instead of imitating a body
double, filmmakers turn to CGI.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.960
First, the crew will shoot the
actor in front of a mirror.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:23.167
Then they will film just the
background behind the actor.

00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:26.820
VFX artists then wipe away the
camera by swapping in pieces

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.210
of the clean plate of
the reflected background.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:30.800
This allows for the kinds of shots

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.030
once thought to be impossible.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:36.070
However, the process isn't
as easy as it sounds.

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:39.190
Take these mirror shots
from 2014's "Birdman."

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:40.920
To perfectly match the background shots,

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:41.753
they had to make sure

00:02:41.753 --> 00:02:44.150
the lighting was identical in each pass,

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.750
and the artists had to be
careful where they cut.

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:49.830
For this one close-up, you'll
see green tape on the camera.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.350
This helped the VFX
artists avoid cutting out

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.150
any of Michael Keaton's hair.

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:57.543
The process gets even trickier
if the camera is moving.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.320
Take this legendary shot
from 1997's "Contact,"

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.870
where one shot seemingly
turns into the reflection

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:06.680
on a medicine cabinet mirror.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:08.880
To make this work,
director Robert Zemeckis

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.896
broke it up into multiple shots

00:03:10.896 --> 00:03:13.562
and replaced the entire mirror with CG.

00:03:13.562 --> 00:03:15.645
First, Zemeckis had actress Jena Malone

00:03:15.645 --> 00:03:19.170
run towards the camera instead
of filming her from behind.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.110
The camera was placed on a Steadicam,

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:23.850
allowing for smooth
shots and repeated takes.

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.090
After running down the hall,

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:27.610
Malone reached for the knob
of the medicine cabinet,

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:29.950
but instead of a mirror,
there was a blue screen.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:32.580
Then the crew shot the
bathroom background on its own

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.200
and placed it as well as
the texture of a mirror

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:37.530
on top of the blue screen.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:39.880
This one shot was so influential

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:42.790
you still see similar
styles decades later,

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:45.597
like in this episode of HBO's "Euphoria."

00:03:48.280 --> 00:03:50.520
All these tricks and more were used

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:53.340
in 2021's "Last Night in Soho."

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:54.920
Production designer Marcus Rowland

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:57.800
and VFX supervisor Tom Proctor of DNEG

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.830
had to build a world of mirrors

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:02.530
while avoiding cameras in the final shot.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.840
The Café de Paris set alone was lined

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:07.710
with an estimated 178 mirrors,

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:10.190
which all needed accurate reflections.

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:13.270
But they had to do much
more than remove the camera,

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:15.700
since Eloise, played by Thomasin McKenzie,

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.210
frequently looks in the
mirror and sees Sandie,

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:21.100
played by Anya Taylor-Joy, and vice versa.

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:24.380
And many of the mirror shots
were incredibly active.

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:27.360
So like "Contact" before
it, they used Steadicams,

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.850
relying on the consistent
movements from Chris Bain

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:34.420
to get shots like this
and this over and over.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:37.010
For key scenes with multiple
multifaceted mirrors

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:38.410
and large camera moves,

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:40.790
the team relied on motion-control cameras.

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:42.690
Since their moves are
programmed in advance,

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.550
it made it easier to do separate takes

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:47.150
of both actresses walking down the stairs.

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:48.275
And to make sure the leads

00:04:48.275 --> 00:04:49.934
matched their movements precisely,

00:04:49.934 --> 00:04:53.530
"Soho" brought in a movement
coach for weeks of preparation.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:55.590
To allow the stars to
interact more directly,

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:58.470
the "Soho" team had to
build several double sets.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:00.430
This shot here, where Eloise first walks

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:03.230
into the Café de Paris and
sees Sandie in the mirror?

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:06.680
The two actresses were actually
facing each other on set.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:08.250
Rowland built a double lobby,

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:11.150
allowing for direct interaction
between the actresses.

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:12.720
And just like in "Terminator 2,"

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:16.140
they used a real set of twins
to tie the illusion together.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:19.120
But the shot could go from
just Eloise's reflection

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:21.490
to Sandie's with no visible cuts

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:24.120
thanks to adjustable mirrors.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.270
When the attendant takes
off Eloise's jacket,

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:28.500
they're standing in
front of a real mirror.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:31.230
Then one of the twins walks
in front of the camera.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:34.320
This gave the crew just enough
time to push the mirror away,

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.820
allowing Sandie to step in.

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:38.990
And as a viewer, you'd never know

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:40.330
there wasn't a mirror there,

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:42.110
thanks to this very subtle bevel

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:44.433
added to the edge of the mirror with CGI.

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:49.770
Every mirror scene presented
its own challenges,

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:52.150
so in some cases, the team had to rely

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:55.110
on two tricks at once,
like here, where Taylor-Joy

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:57.320
stands in front of a
mirror with Matt Smith,

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:59.940
whose real reflection
is caught in the mirror.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:02.090
Smith is in front of a real mirror,

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:04.970
but the rest of the glass is CGI.

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:05.877
For the final touches,

00:06:05.877 --> 00:06:08.940
"Soho" utilized the most
subtle VFX improvements

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:11.250
to avoid any continuity errors.

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:12.860
For example, Proctor and his team

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:15.190
inserted green-screen shots of extras

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:17.210
into many final shots to cover up

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:19.780
the rough edges of where
a camera was cut out,

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:21.680
and for any mismatched mirror movements,

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:24.850
visual effects artists used
digital re-timing tools.

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:26.730
They could get into shots frame by frame

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:28.200
and individually speed up

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.380
or slow down individual characters,

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:32.905
bringing them into perfect alignment.

